<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yue Fei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1103–1142</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yue Fei was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> general who led campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a> and its client state of Qi in the 1130s, recovering territory north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. Opposed to the peace policy of Emperor Gaozong and the chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> Hui, he was recalled, imprisoned and executed around the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shaoxing">Treaty of Shaoxing</a>. He later became a national hero symbolising loyalty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yue-fei.jpg" alt="Yue Fei" width="320" height="669" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liu Songnian · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B2%B3%E9%A3%9B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jin forces pursued the new Southern Song court south of the Yangtze in 1129–1130 but were checked by Song generals such as Han Shizhong and <b>Yue Fei</b>, and the Jin governed part of the conquered land through the puppet state of Qi from 1130 to 1137.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the 10th-century generals who had made and unmade dynasties, the Song placed the army under civil officials: civilians headed the Bureau of Military Affairs and commanded on the front line, commanders were moved after each campaign, and successful generals were distrusted, as <b>Yue Fei</b>, executed in 1142, discovered.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li><li><blockquote>With Jin support his armies attacked Southern Song several times between 1133 and 1136, but were defeated by Song generals including <b>Yue Fei</b>, and Qi lost credibility with its masters.</blockquote><a href="/state/qi-1130-1137">Qi (1130-1137)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Shaoxing in 1141 recognised the Huai river as the frontier, and the general <b>Yue Fei</b>, who had opposed peace, was executed soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-song">Southern Song</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tangyin County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.92,114.36&amp;year=1128" aria-label="Tangyin County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7434 867 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yue Fei"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7884 1137h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7884 1137h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.92,114.36&amp;year=1128">What was here in 1128? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;On being mounted, they did not immediately break into a gallop; but would gradually warm into eagerness for their work.&#8221;</p><footer>Good Horses, his answer to the emperor (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; of his two old horses: a parable of men well and badly used &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Such is the capacity for endurance in those who are well fed and well treated; who are willing, but not over-zealous.&#8221;</p><footer>Good Horses, his answer to the emperor (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Such is the jaded condition of those who get little and are easily satisfied, who are over-eager and are easily exhausted.&#8221;</p><footer>Good Horses, his answer to the emperor (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
